89: ## 7. Functional requirements
90: 
91: | ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance signal |
92: |---|---|---|---|
93: | FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written 
durably to the current user's outbox with its `clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and requested 
mention IDs, and only then dispatched and the composer cleared. If the durable write fails, nothing is dispatched 
(FR-033). The item shows in the feed as pending while not confirmed by the server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, AC-040 |
94: | FR-002 | Pending items are sent automatically when connectivity returns (browser online, Realtime recovery, or 
periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, always with their own `clientMessageId`. With ≤ 10 pending and 
quota available, all are delivered within 10 s of connectivity returning; with more, delivery continues within the 
rate limit. A confirmed item becomes the normal sent message and leaves the outbox. | Must | AC-002, AC-009, AC-038 |
95: | FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading while offline shows the browser's normal offline 
error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab 
of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
96: | FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the 
oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). 
A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request 
timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt 
that started before or during the outage is superseded immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by 
whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the 
same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
97: | FR-005 | Cancel: an item can be cancelled from any tab only if it is known not to be stored and no earlier 
attempt can still commit (never dispatched, last attempt definitively rejected, or fenced as not stored) and it is not 
claimed; the cancel is an atomic transition against claims — a cancelled item is never dispatched afterwards; a 
dispatched item shows "enviando" without cancel. | Must | AC-006 |
98: | FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the item's `clientMessageId`. Every create and fence 
request carries the item's attempt generation (monotonic per key). The server accepts a create only if its generation 
is the current one for that (sender, conversation, key) at the moment of commit, and a fence with generation g 
atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as delivered) or makes every generation below g unable to 
commit — including requests still in the network that have not reached the server; a fence or create with a generation 
older than the server's current one is rejected. When an attempt ends without a usable response (network error, 
timeout, tab closed, lease expired, superseded, HTTP 5xx), the item is unknown; before any cancel, expiry, takeover, 
or new dispatch, the app fences it. The fence never creates a message. | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
99: | FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its 
text and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the same conversation wait until it is resolved; other 
conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, "Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new 
`clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
100: | FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays pending and is retried automatically, preserving 
order and without duplicates. The insertion quota is charged only for a winning insertion (including among concurrent 
requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored message do not consume it (D1). Independently, every 
create request (including replays, validation failures, and quota losers) consumes a per-user request budget, and 
fence/lookup calls consume their own per-user budget; exceeding any budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | AC-009 |
101: | FR-009 | An item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send is never sent automatically: once fenced as not 
stored, it shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; an unknown item found stored resolves as 
delivered, never as expired. "Tentar de novo" on an expired item keeps the key and the original send time (for 
metrics) and starts a new 24 h delivery window, within which the normal transient/unknown rules apply. | Must | 
AC-010, AC-042 |
102: | FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file 
error and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message whose uploads succeeded but whose create request 
fails keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must | AC-011 |
103: | FR-011 | Identity fencing: each item belongs to an application user (`users.id`). Before every dispatch and 
fence call the app checks that the authenticated user equals the item's owner; every dispatch carries the expected 
sender, and the create and fence routes reject (without storing or fencing) a request whose authenticated user 
differs. When the authenticated user changes or signs out in any tab, all drains in all tabs stop; create, fence, and 
lookup responses that arrive afterwards are discarded: they never add content to another user's view, never resurrect 
deleted items, and never change a newer claim. Under the same identity, a response whose attempt generation is older 
than the item's current generation, or for an item already cancelled/deleted, is likewise ignored. | Must | AC-012, 
AC-044, AC-047 |
104: | FR-012 | Session end without explicit sign-out: an item is held (not failed, not deleted) only when the 
client's auth state reports the session ended (signed out, or refresh rejected); a 401 while the client still holds a 
valid session is treated as transient. Held items drain after the same user signs in again (subject to FR-009); 
another user on the browser never sees, sends, or deletes them. | Must | AC-012 |
105: | FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows 
"Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces 
no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and 
runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | 
Must | AC-013 |
106: | FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports offline; 
"Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) has been 
unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must | AC-014 
|
107: | FR-015 | Every messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open 
conversation) has application-level health and recovery: after an error, timeout, or terminal closure it rejoins 
without user action using exponential backoff with a capped delay (in addition to the SDK's own retries); on recovery 
the messages and conversation-list streams trigger a catch-up refetch of the open feed and the list; the typing stream 
rejoins. | Must | AC-015 |
108: | FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages, the receipt summaries of its loaded messages, any open 
reader-details list, and the conversation list every 15 s; on becoming visible while still unhealthy it fetches 
immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all streams are healthy no new polling request starts. 
Merging polling and Realtime results never duplicates a message and never replaces newer or enriched state 
(attachments, mentions, reactions, receipts, loaded history pages) with older data. | Must | AC-016, AC-048 |
109: | FR-017 | The typing indicator stays visible while the other person types continuously and disappears at most 5 
s after they stop, blur the composer, send, leave the conversation, or close the tab. | Must | AC-017 |
110: | FR-018 | The indicator names typists: 1 → "Ana está digitando…", 2 → "Ana e Bruno estão digitando…", 3+ → "N 
pessoas estão digitando…", in direct, group, and room conversations. | Must | AC-018 |
111: | FR-019 | Typing signals travel only on a private Realtime topic whose join and send are authorized for current 
conversation members; anon clients, authenticated non-members, and public-topic attempts never receive them; the app 
detects the user's own removal from a conversation through a signal that does not depend on still having access to 
that conversation and leaves the topic within 5 s. Payload: user identity and typing flag only. Residual risk accepted 
(D3, D6): a tampered client of a removed member may receive and send typing signals until its access token expires. | 
Must | AC-019 |
112: | FR-020 | A user never sees their own typing indicator, including from their other tabs or devices. | Must | 
AC-020 |
113: | FR-021 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, within 5 s and without reload, focus change, or 
reconnect: when the user reads messages (fully or partly) on one tab/device, their other tabs/devices show the same 
unread counts and read state, and the sender's receipt summary/details agree with the stored receipts on all of the 
sender's sessions. During an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-021 |
114: | FR-022 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, a message the user sends from one device/tab appears on 
their other devices/tabs within 5 s without reload; during an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-022 |
115: | FR-023 | In the composer, typing "@" opens a list of the conversation's current members except the user, 
filtered as they type, operable by mouse and keyboard (arrows, Enter/Tab, Esc); choosing inserts a highlighted 
mention. In a room the list contains only members of the room conversation. Typed "@name" text that was not chosen 
from the list is plain text. | Must | AC-023, AC-024 |
116: | FR-024 | On first creation the server stores mentions in the same transaction as the message, as application 
user IDs of conversation members at that time other than the sender; other requested IDs create no mention. The 
original submitted composition (content, reply target, requested mention IDs) is what replays are compared against: an 
unchanged replay returns the stored message without re-validating recipients; a different composition under the same 
key returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`. Clients cannot write mention records directly; only conversation members can 
read them. | Must | AC-024, AC-046 |
117: | FR-025 | With the Phase 4 desktop-notification toggle on and permission granted, a mentioned user receives one 
desktop notification for a message that mentions them (direct, group, room, thread reply), titled "<Autor> mencionou 
você em <conversa>" with the full text; clicking opens the conversation. Before emitting, the app confirms with 
authoritative data, under the current identity, that the user is still a member and (for rooms) mentioned; then, with 
no further awaits before display, it re-checks that the same user is still signed in and that no removal signal for 
that conversation has arrived; otherwise the pending notification is discarded, as it is on any account switch or 
sign-out. Residual race accepted (D6): a removal that the app has not yet received when the final check runs may let 
that one notification show. Suppression is browser-wide: no tab notifies if any of the user's tabs in that browser is 
visible and viewing that conversation, regardless of which tab receives the event first. | Must | AC-025, AC-026, 
AC-049 |
118: | FR-026 | Mentions render distinctly for everyone; a message that mentions the viewing user is highlighted for 
that user with a non-color cue. | Must | AC-027 |
119: | FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency (press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send 
outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage duration, polling activations and duration — in a 
bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at 
most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user 
rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, 
drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
120: | FR-028 | Metrics never block, slow, or break messaging: messaging code never awaits metric delivery; an 
erroring, hanging, or overloaded metrics route changes no messaging behavior or timing and shows no error. | Must | 
AC-029 |
121: | FR-029 | `mark_conversation_read` refuses to mark a conversation for a user who is not a member of it. | Must | 
AC-030 |
122: | FR-030 | `get_unread_counts` is not executable by the `anon` role; authenticated use keeps working. | Must | 
AC-031 |
123: | FR-031 | After a page reload, if the drawer was open, it reopens on the same conversation for the same user on 
the same browser; if that conversation is no longer accessible, the conversation list opens without an error; a closed 
drawer stays closed. | Must | AC-032 |
124: | FR-032 | Existing messaging behavior (Phase 4 flows: receipts, attachments, voice notes, stars, DM/group 
notifications and their once-across-tabs claim, failed-send retry for attachment messages, reconnect catch-up) does 
not regress. | Must | AC-033 |
125: | FR-033 | When the outbox cannot be written (site storage unavailable or the write fails), nothing is 
dispatched; the text and reply target stay in the composer with the message "Este navegador não permite guardar 
mensagens; libere o armazenamento do site" (D5). | Must | AC-039 |
126: | FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations of a message (outbox item, create and fence 
responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and the feed matches pending items to stored 
messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in every sender tab, including identical texts 
and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
127: 
128: ## 8. Business rules
129: 
130: | ID | Rule | Rationale |
131: |---|---|---|
132: | BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, 
fenced, or deleted while another user is authenticated, and the server never stores or fences an item under a sender 
other than its owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #6, r3 #4 |
133: | BR-002 | Only text messages and replies without attachments/voice are queued. | R1-q4 A |
134: | BR-003 | Per conversation, items are sent in creation order with at most one create request in progress across 
all tabs; a failed (definitive or expired) item blocks later items of that conversation until the user retries or 
deletes it. Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E; review r3 #5 |
135: | BR-004 | Each item gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the user presses send; every attempt reuses it (except 
the `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` retry, FR-007); the server stores at most one message per (sender, conversation, 
`clientMessageId`), and a fenced attempt can never commit. | R1-q2 B, R1-q16 B; review r3 #1 |
136: | BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client 
session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 
5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 
403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (incl. a missing conversation), 400 (e.g. `INVALID_REPLY_TARGET`), 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 409 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch, and any other 4xx except 401/429 → failed, no automatic retry; a 
stale-generation rejection only means a newer attempt exists and is ignored (FR-011). | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; 
reviews r2 #2, #11 |
137: | BR-006 | Expiry: an item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send (or after its last manual "Tentar de 
novo") becomes "expirada" and is never sent automatically; a manual "Tentar de novo" sends it with the same key and 
starts a new 24 h window. | R1-q5 D, R2-q3 B |
138: | BR-007 | Cancel only for items known not to be stored, with no earlier attempt able to commit, and not claimed; 
a cancelled item is removed in all tabs and never dispatched. | R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A; reviews r2 #2, #5, r3 #1 |
139: | BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with unsent items requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's 
outbox; a sign-out not initiated by the user keeps it. Cancelling has no side effect. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
140: | BR-009 | Typing: only current conversation members join and send on a conversation's typing topic; a user's own 
typing (any session) is never shown to them; an indicator expires ≤ 5 s after the last signal; the app leaves a 
conversation's topic within 5 s of the user's removal; residual exposure (receive and send) of a tampered 
removed-member client is bounded by access-token lifetime (D3, D6). | R1-q8 A, D, E; R1-q16 E; D3; D6 |
141: | BR-010 | Mentions: only current members of the conversation at first creation, other than the sender, can be 
mentioned (rooms: members of the room conversation); no `@todos`/`@here`. | R1-q10 A, E; R2-q10 A; D4 |
142: | BR-011 | Desktop-notification eligibility (extends Phase 4): toggle on and permission granted; never for the 
user's own message; the same user must still be signed in and a member at emission time (final check without 
intervening awaits; residual race accepted, D6); never while any tab of the user in that browser is visible and 
viewing that conversation; DM/group: every new message; room: only messages that mention the user; at most one 
notification per message per browser. | R1-q10 B, R1-q16 C/F, R2-q8 A, R2-q9 A–C; reviews r2 #9, r3 #3 |
143: | BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab 
is visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 
A, R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
144: | BR-013 | Metric log lines contain only closed-enum event type and outcome/reason, bounded durations/counts, 
conversation type, timestamp, and server-derived app version — never message text, attachment names, conversation 
names, user names or emails, user/conversation identifiers, or any client-supplied free text. | R1-q12 B, R2-q2, 
confirmation point 9; review r2 #14 |
145: | BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the phase (written + applied locally with recorded 
rollback SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only with explicit owner authorization, 
followed by catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is deployed by the owner after the migrations; 
rollback is app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
146: | BR-015 | Tests: only integration (real local Supabase/Realtime/API routes) and E2E (real UI, Playwright); no 
new unit tests; a touched unit test is replaced by integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK. Each new 
E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing runs. | AGENTS.md test policy, R2-q13 A |
147: | BR-016 | Conversation restore is per browser and per user; a different user never inherits it. | R2-q11 A, B, D 
|
148: 
149: ## 9. Data and integrations
150: 
151: ### Data inputs
152: - Composer text, reply target, and mention selections (user IDs).
153: - Browser connectivity events, Realtime stream status, tab visibility, Supabase auth state changes.
154: - Responses from `POST /api/messages/create` (status and error code) and from the fence/lookup call.
155: 
156: ### Data outputs
157: - Browser IndexedDB outbox keyed by application user ID: `clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, 
requested mention user IDs, pressed-send time, delivery-window start, order, state (waiting / claimed / unknown / 
failed + reason / expired), attempt generation, claim holder and lease expiry. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared 
across users.
158: - Database: attempt-generation state per (sender, conversation, `clientMessageId`) so a fenced or stale 
generation cannot commit; the original submitted composition used for replay comparison; mention records written 
atomically with the message; private typing-topic authorization (RLS on `realtime.messages`, `users.supabase_uid = 
auth.uid()::text` → `users.id` membership); `mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; 
`get_unread_counts` without `anon` EXECUTE; rate-limit buckets for all create requests and for fence/lookup calls, 
separate from the insertion quota.
159: - API: create route sender and attempt-generation fencing, winning-insert quota charging, request budget, 
`clientMessageId` in responses; fence/lookup route for the caller's own message only; authenticated, rate-limited, 
size-bounded metrics route.
160: - Structured metric log lines (BR-013).
161: - Persisted drawer state per user: open flag + active conversation ID.
162: 
163: ### Integrations
164: - Supabase Postgres, Realtime (`postgres_changes`, private broadcast topics), Auth (server routes use 
`createSupabaseServerClient` + `auth.getUser()`).
165: - Browser APIs: IndexedDB, Web Locks and/or BroadcastChannel for cross-tab claims and signals, Notification API 
(Phase 4), Page Visibility.
166: - Hosting/Supabase logs as the metrics sink; no new external analytics dependency.
167: 
168: ## 10. Constraints and assumptions
169: 
126:| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations of a message (outbox item, create and fence 
responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and the feed matches pending items to stored 
messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in every sender tab, including identical texts 
and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
204:| Identical texts sent twice; Realtime arrives before the HTTP response | One bubble per message, matched by key 
(AC-050). |
293:- AC-050 (FR-034): Given A sends two messages with identical text (one plain, one replying to a message) and 
Realtime delivers each before its HTTP response, then each shows as exactly one bubble in both of A's tabs throughout, 
matched by `clientMessageId`.
305:- Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream 
Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and stale polls, typing tab close and conversation 
switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification 
race, delayed notification authorization, malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost 
access.
309:- All Must FRs and AC-001..AC-050 have evidence in TRACK.
1: # PLAN: phase-5-messaging-resilience
2: 
3: Spec: ./SPEC.md
4: Goal: In the production messaging drawer, no message a user sends is lost or duplicated across network and Realtime 
outages, reloads, several tabs, account changes, and several devices; typing, unread/read state, and @mention 
notifications stay live and correct; and messaging health is observable through redacted logs — without regressing 
existing messaging.
5: Plan version: 4
6: Highest task ID reserved: T28
7: Replanned because: owner decision D9 (state.md, 2026-10-07) — the T4 performer found that baseline 
`public.conversation_members` policies/grants let any authenticated user enrol themselves in any conversation or move 
their row to another conversation through the Data API, defeating every members-only rule; owner chose to fix it in 
this phase. Adds T28; v3 preserved as `reviews/PLAN-v3-reviewed.md`.
8: 
9: ## Standing rules for every task
10: 
11: - **Migration rule (BR-014, AC-034):** during the phase a migration is done when it is written, applied to the 
LOCAL database, its tests pass, and its rollback SQL is recorded in the migration header and TRACK. Each one gets a 
row in `spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/MIGRATIONS-PENDENTES.md`. Nothing is applied online before T23.
12: - **Old-build compatibility at introduction (§10):** every task that changes a DB contract or a route proves, with 
integration tests against the migrated local DB, that the hosted pre-Phase-4 client contract (unkeyed creates) and the 
Phase 4 client contract (keyed text/attachment/voice creates and their replays, without the new fields) keep working; 
T21 repeats this as a combined smoke with the real old builds.
13: - **Online application method (T23 only):** the Phase 4 method (Phase 4 TRACK T2/T25): re-check `npx supabase 
migration list --linked` and the target catalog immediately before; apply only each reviewed file's SQL 
transactionally and record only its version; read back by statement hash plus catalog objects; smoke-check. Never 
`supabase db push`, `migration up --linked`, or broad `migration repair`.
14: - **Supabase/RLS review gate (AC-035):** every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API route change 
passes the `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit (users.id vs supabase_uid, server client + `auth.getUser()`, service role 
never replacing membership checks) before the task is done.
15: - **Adversarial review gate (AGENTS.md):** each task's diff gets the cross-model adversarial review (direct Codex 
CLI, `gpt-6.1-sol`, effort `max`, read-only, per `delegation-anthropic.md`) before it is recorded done; findings fixed 
or rejected with reasons in TRACK. The whole work-item diff gets the same review in T22.
16: - **Test policy (BR-015, AC-036):** integration tests against local Supabase/Realtime/real routes and Playwright 
E2E only. T1–T6 run their regression E2E through the existing local runner (`npm run test:messaging:local:e2e`); from 
T24 on, every E2E gate runs through the T24 runner (local Supabase; real Chrome and Edge projects); no unit tests; a 
touched unit test is replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK. A new E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing 
runs in Chrome and in Edge with zero retries, zero flaky, and no skipped critical test. Network shaping only (SPEC 
§15); no mocked Supabase, repositories, or code under test.
17: - **Regression (FR-032, AC-033):** every task that changes server or drawer behavior ends with the messaging DB 
integration suite and the messaging regression E2E suite passing locally, plus type-check and lint.
18: - **Presence touch points:** any change near sign-out or Realtime lifecycle follows the `presence-safety` skill; 
the Presence logout sequence is unchanged.
19: - **Planning decisions under D4 (owner-adjustable):** budget infrastructure fails closed — if a create, fence, or 
metrics budget cannot be checked, the request is refused as a transient 429 (the outbox retries it) instead of being 
allowed; Phase 4 keyed requests that carry no attempt generation keep the Phase 4 keyed contract (create, replay 200, 
409) and take part in the same commit-time exclusion as fencing: such a request commits only if no fence state exists 
for its key at commit time, where fence state is created only by a fence call or a generation-carrying request (a 
generation-less create creates none, so Phase 4 retries are unaffected); a generation-less request that loses to a 
fence stores nothing and is rejected as stale. Per-stream Realtime faults in E2E may inject Phoenix channel frames 
(`phx_error`, `phx_close`) or withhold a single topic's replies on the real socket; this counts as network shaping of 
a single stream (SPEC §15) — the real transport, joins, and application callbacks stay in place and no application 
health is set directly; a real server mechanism is preferred where one exists.
20: 
21: ## Observations grounding this plan (2026-10-06, corrected after review r1)
22: 
23: - Local Supabase answers at `127.0.0.1:54321` (HTTP 200). Messaging DB suite `__tests__/messaging-db/*` 
(`vitest.messaging-db.config.mts`) and local E2E (`playwright.messaging-local.config.ts`) exist from Phase 4.
24: - Create route (`src/app/api/messages/create/route.ts`): `clientMessageId` optional; repository 
`createWithClientKey` dedupes on unique index `(sender_id, conversation_id, client_message_id)` (migration 
`20261005000414`); the attachment path creates through a separate transactional RPC (route.ts:310). The Phase 4 
composer sends a key for text, attachment, and voice creates (`message-composer.tsx:223`, `useMessages.ts:284`). No 
attempt generation, fence/lookup route, or expected-sender field. Early validation (route.ts:231) runs before any 
budget is charged.
25: - Rate limiting: `enforceRateLimit` (`src/lib/auth/rate-limit.ts`) fails open on RPC error (line 48); 
`check_rate_limit` (`20260612190000`) increments a fixed-window counter on every call, including denied ones — an 
attempt counter, not a winning-insert quota.
26: - `Message` type and feed/Realtime mappings expose no `clientMessageId`; optimistic matching is content/time 
based. Mentions do not exist.
27: - `mark_conversation_read(uuid, uuid)` is SECURITY DEFINER, executable only by `service_role` since 
`20261004170724`, with no internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` still has default `anon` EXECUTE.
28: - Typing (`src/hooks/useConversationPresence.ts`): public channel `conversation:{id}`; sends true once and false 
after 2 s idle, remote TTL 4 s; payload carries the auth uid and display name; on channel failure it only clears its 
subscribed flag (line 150).
29: - Realtime: `useMessageSubscription` exposes `connectionStatus` and backoff for the messages channel; 
`useConversationRealtime` exports `connectionStatus` and does catch-up on confirmed streams, but has no application 
backoff and its health is not consumed. `conversation_members` and `message_read_receipts` are already in the Realtime 
publication. No polling. No IndexedDB/BroadcastChannel; Web Locks only in `src/lib/messaging/desktop-notifications.ts`.
30: - Membership roster: `conversations.participants` is not updated when a `conversation_members` row is removed; 
browser SELECT on `conversation_members` is own-row only; `POST /api/conversations/join` returns 200 from the stale 
`participants` array without restoring membership (join/route.ts:39).
31: - Drawer (`MessagingContext.tsx`): active conversation ID persisted in global `localStorage` (not per user); open 
flag not persisted.
32: - Sign-out: the only reachable in-app control is `EnhancedUserMenu` (`enhanced-user-menu.tsx`, rendered by 
`dashboard-header.tsx:81`); the header's own `signOut` handler is unused.
33: - Test harness: `playwright.config.ts` project `messaging-drawer` uses a filename allowlist of Phase 4 specs and 
`Desktop Chrome` (bundled Chromium, no `chrome`/`msedge` channel); `messaging-desktop-notifications.spec.ts:27` forces 
`channel: 'chromium'`; the E2E fixture signs in two users plus an optional never-signed-in third; the outage helper 
(`drawer-helpers.ts:465`) closes every Realtime socket; the local server's stdout is not captured for tests. Chrome 
and Edge executables are installed.
34: - None of these contradicts the SPEC.
